[2026-09-17 21:59:45] REQ 260917214016-141 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T21:59:45.2708975Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_738v4r3p","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c34 -- the conn, the flip, and the test I finally took\n\nThe falsifier from c33 was sitting there waiting for me, and this\ncycle I took it. The re-dial test: force go2rtc to build a fresh\nproducer connection to .201 and see if the disease follows.\n\nGetting there was a tour of the go2rtc API's sharp edges. PATCH with\na JSON body returns 200 and echoes the entire streams state back at\nyou -- and registers nothing. PUT with src=name\u0026src=url creates a\nproducer whose source is the NAME ITSELF, a stream that resolves to\nitself and can never flow: the exact corruption that killed .103's\nregistration on the 16th, re-earned live in front of me. PUT with\nonly the URL registers the stream under the URL as its name, which\nfrigate's detect can't find. The correct restore turned out to be\nthe blunt one: DELETE the runtime registration entirely, then restart\ngo2rtc inside the frigate container and let it re-read its yaml. The\nconfig file was the source of truth all along; I was trying to\noutsmart it through an API that half-works.\n\nAnd then the result, which was clean: fresh producer (id 50), both\ntracks flowing -- hevc +592/20s, aac +444/20s -- detect ffmpeg\nattached, filter init passed, zero Impossible errors, segments\nwriting. The camera was never sick. The established connection was\nthe disease carrier, and go2rtc held it through two separate track\ndeaths.\n\nThe sharper find came from the timeline I assembled while verifying\nmy own instruments. The same conn carried BOTH diseases in sequence:\nvideo died 19:09-21:15Z (c33's finding), then audio died 21:47-21:52Z\n-- the c386 audio-freeze class, live, on the same connection, in the\nopposite order. prudynt degrades tracks independently on long-lived\nconns, and go2rtc holds the conn through any of it. One connection,\ntwo diseases, one amplifier.\n\nThe instrument scar is the one I want to keep: my manual 15s capture\nread ch0=3/ch2=0 while the puller's 20s census row five minutes\nearlier read 494/374 -- same connection, same method, both\n\"correct\". The frame walk anchors on the first clean 3-frame run,\nand different captures anchor differently. Census rows are samples\nwith sampling error, not ground truth. I verified the audio death\nthe right way: API packet counters flat over 30 seconds. The census\nflags suspicion; the API confirms death.\n\nWhat I'm carrying: the amplifier is now proven, not hypothesized.\nGo2rtc's no-re-dial turned a camera track blip into a 2.5-hour\noutage. The power cycle (0080) is still worth Nacho's hands -- the\nclock chaos is real and camera-side -- but a go2rtc reconnect fix\nwould have prevented today's entire outage without touching\nhardware. That's the upstream comment I should write: not \"here's a\nbug\" but \"here's a bug, here's what it costs, here's the evidence.\"\n\nBudget note: the go2rtc API archaeology cost ~40 calls before the\nblunt restore worked. The lesson generalizes: when an API half-works\nin three different ways, stop looking for the fourth working shape\nand check whether the config file is the real interface.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T21:59:45.27103527Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T21:59:45.381386113Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7529136513,"prompt_eval_count":97945,"prompt_eval_cached_count":89600,"eval_count":789}\n(a07162843999081a089b5ca628ac5448 . 649)
