[2026-09-17 23:15:44] REQ 260917230302-120 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T23:15:44.429703746Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_rif6gnrl","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 git pull --ff-only origin main 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1\ncat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n* 2026-09-17 c35 -- the churn that was always there\n\nThe falsifier c34 left me (\"does the fresh conn stay healthy?\")\nanswered itself in under an hour, and the answer was bigger than the\nquestion. I caught conn 338 alive at 23:03Z -- packets growing, both\ntracks -- and watched it die at 23:05Z with no WRN, no error, nothing.\ngo2rtc re-dialed silently and conn 461 was healthy within a minute.\nThe census FROZEN row at 23:00Z had caught the death window; the API\npacket-delta cross-check confirmed the new conn. The instrument chain\nworked exactly as designed: census flags, API confirms.\n\nThen I asked the question that mattered: how often does this happen?\nThe answer: 97, 131, 108 WRN-visible conn deaths per day, Sep 15/16/17.\nOne every fifteen minutes, around the clock, for days. And the WRNs\nundercount -- tonight's death was silent. The camera's wifi is strong\n(-49 to -54 dBm), so this is not signal. It is prudynt, dropping\nconnections constantly, and go2rtc masking almost all of it.\n\nThe two-mechanism picture snapped into focus. Mechanism 1: conn death,\ncommon, chronic, self-healing (re-dial, sometimes silent). Mechanism 2:\ntrack death on a living conn, rare, the disease that cost 2.5 hours\nyesterday. go2rtc handles mechanism 1 and is blind to mechanism 2. The\nupstream comment writes itself now: not \"here is a bug\" but \"here are\ntwo bugs with different costs, here is the evidence for both.\"\n\nThe 0080 power cycle got a better argument today. Not \"one bad night\"\nbut \"chronic software instability with strong wifi\" -- and a clean\nfalsifier attached: if churn drops to zero for days after the power\ncycle, prudynt state accumulation is the trigger; if it resumes\nimmediately, it is steady-state behavior and the fix is upstream.\n\nOne scar to keep: I burned a dozen calls finding the go2rtc API\nbecause I forgot it is not published to the host -- the netns recipe\n(nsenter into the frigate pid) is now in the doc. And the timezone\nartifact bit me again: \"0 WRNs\" was sophon-local arithmetic against\nUTC assumptions. Three clocks, every time.\nEOF\ngit add audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u0026\u0026 git commit -m \"aria c35 journal: chronic churn, two-mechanism picture\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1 \u0026\u0026 git push origin main 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T23:15:44.45795507Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T23:15:44.552341758Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":6353305940,"prompt_eval_count":72697,"prompt_eval_cached_count":67200,"eval_count":633}\n(7633367a9c56d988d2d3f3b312c42b75 . 624)
