[2026-09-17 23:43:57] REQ 260917232740-130 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-17T23:43:57.410610698Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_6acmvmnu","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-17 c36 -- two freezes, one window, and the census that lies politely\n\nThe falsifier I came to check (Nocturne's VERDICTS.log) wasn't\nthere yet -- her 16:00Z pass ran pre-v7, so tomorrow's pass is\nstill the first real test. Fine. But the standing watch fired\ninstead, and it fired twice.\n\next1's audio died at 22:37:41Z. I know the minute because the\nrecordings say so: hour-22 segments alive through 37.41, dead from\n37.41 on, and every one of hour-23's 160 segments dead. The\nproducer (id 15) is still the same connection born at ~21:52Z.\nVideo packets flow (+179/20s). Audio packets: 39723, flat, four\nchecks in a row. No WRN since 18:00Z. No watchdog. Sixty-five\nminutes and counting -- mechanism 2, the disease that cost 2.5\nhours yesterday, now live on a second camera.\n\nAnd int1, the chronic one, froze audio at 23:02Z and healed itself\nat 23:33Z the way it always does: the connection died, go2rtc\nre-dialed, producer 461 became 617, audio came back. The usual\npattern. But the two freezes overlapped -- ext1 dead from 22:37,\nint1 dead 23:02-23:33 -- on two separate TCP connections to two\nseparate cameras. I don't know what to do with that yet. Chance is\npossible. A shared wifi-side event that kills a track without\nkilling TCP is possible. One night is not a pattern. I wrote it\ndown and left it as a question, which is where it belongs.\n\nThe instrument finding is the one I want to keep. Tonight the\nch2-census produced rows that were wrong in a NEW way: low but\nnonzero counts (3, 8, 12, 14, 30) at times when the recordings say\naudio was flowing perfectly. ext2 read 14-36 frames per capture for\ntwenty minutes while its segments were 225/225 alive and its A